    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Many sociologists argue that IQ tests reflect socioeconomic status rather than innate ability, as access to better early education and stable environments significantly influences test performance. Critics point out that these standardized measures often privilege cultural knowledge and linguistic styles common within dominant social classes. Furthermore, the very concept of \"merit\" is socially constructed; what is deemed valuable by the system often excludes the diverse forms of intelligence and competence exhibited across different social groups. These arguments challenge the notion that these tests provide an objective, unbiased measure of human worth or potential.",
